TESTDATA["pet6-t2"] = {"title":"Cambridge PET 6 · Test 2","format":"2010","reading":[{"part":1,"items":[{"n":1,"text":"","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["This shop will sell customers’ watches within twelve months.","This shop will keep customers’ watches for up to twelve months.","This shop will look after customers’ watches for more than twelve months."],"answer":"B"},{"n":2,"text":"","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["Philippe and Stefano missed each other at the stadium.","Stefano had to leave without Philippe to get to work.","Stefano has given up waiting for Philippe to arrive."],"answer":"C"},{"n":3,"text":"","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["Parents must return forms this week if their child is going on Friday’s trip.","Parents cannot go on next month’s trip unless they return their forms by Friday.","The last day for returning completed forms for the trip is Friday."],"answer":"C"},{"n":4,"text":"","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"Gabi wants Jo to","options":["change an arrangement.","cancel a regular event.","come to a business meeting."],"answer":"A"},{"n":5,"text":"","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["It is not possible to use the lift above the ground floor today.","The lift will not be going to the basement today.","The stairs between the basement and the ground floor are closed today."],"answer":"B"}]},{"part":2,"people":[{"n":6,"name":"Isabel","text":"Isabel works in the city centre and likes painting and drawing in her free time. She wants to practise her hobby in a small, quiet park near her office.","answer":"F"},{"n":7,"name":"Mr Martin","text":"Mr Martin wants to take his eight-year-old pupils to a park anywhere within the city, with lots of organised activities which allow the children to read about local wildlife they may see.","answer":"H"},{"n":8,"name":"Kumiko and Atsuko","text":"Kumiko and Atsuko would like to visit a park which they can get to by boat. They want to buy lunch there and then enjoy a short walk accompanied by an expert leader.","answer":"E"},{"n":9,"name":"Hans and Birgit Kaufmann","text":"Hans and Birgit Kaufmann and their family want to visit a park which is historically important. Their teenage children would like to try a water sport.","answer":"G"},{"n":10,"name":"Melanie and Stefan","text":"Melanie and Stefan are students who need to visit a busy park for a college project. They want to draw people taking part in team sports and watching entertainment.","answer":"C"}],"texts":[{"letter":"A","title":"Hadley Park","text":"This park is in the peaceful village of Cranford, 20 km outside the city. The park has large green spaces for football and there is also an area of woodland, a boating lake, fish ponds and a variety of local wildlife. The public car park is free."},{"letter":"B","title":"Highdown Park","text":"The largest and most popular open space in the city, Highdown has many paths for keen walkers, as well as horse-riding and golf. The much-visited 19th-century glasshouses contain an interesting exhibition about birds from around the world."},{"letter":"C","title":"Brook Park","text":"A beautiful park on the edge of the city, Brook Park attracts huge crowds. The open-air theatre has a programme of plays suitable for school groups. There is a well-used basketball court and baseball field, a children’s playground and a café. Climb Harry’s Hill to admire the beautiful fields and forests beyond the city."},{"letter":"D","title":"Lilac Park and House","text":"This busy city-centre park has a long history dating back to the 1700s, when it belonged to the writer Thomas Crane. The house is open to the public and a guided visit can also include a walk around the famous rose gardens, finishing at the popular Butterfly Café."},{"letter":"E","title":"Boscawen Park","text":"This small and peaceful park offers guided tours, given by the knowledgeable Environment Officers, and evening visitors to the park may be lucky enough to see rare frogs and bats. It is situated on the River Elton and can be reached in about 30 minutes from the city centre by river taxi. There is a snack bar and gift shop."},{"letter":"F","title":"East Bank Park","text":"This is a tiny, little-known park in the heart of the city with gardens filled with sculptures, trees and flowers. It makes a perfect resting place, popular with local artists, and is within minutes of the theatre and entertainment district."},{"letter":"G","title":"Victoria Park","text":"This quiet park, on the edge of the city and easy to visit by public transport, has boats for hire on the lake, a skateboard park, basketball and tennis courts and a picnic area. Often seen in postcard views of the city, Victoria Park contains one of the oldest windmills in the country – the museum should not be missed."},{"letter":"H","title":"Elmwood Park","text":"At Elmwood Park, there are walks on well-made paths and cycle rides for all abilities. Elmwood is just inside the city limit and has an area of quiet woodland, which is home to deer and other animals. The visitor centre, numerous display boards and a fun quiz make this a positive learning experience for all ages."}]},{"part":3,"title":"The Last Great Race on Earth","text":"In the 1880s, gold was discovered in what is now the most northern state of the USA, Alaska. Many people came to the area hoping to get rich. New towns were built and grew quickly. One such town was called Iditarod, which means ‘far, distant place’. This town grew so quickly during 1909 that it briefly became the largest city in Alaska. In the summer months, essential supplies could be delivered to these towns by boat, but in the winter the rivers and the ocean froze and there was no way to get to them. So, in 1910, a winter track was built which would be used by teams of dogs pulling sleds. They would take mail, food and clothes to the gold miners.\n\nThe track became known as the Iditarod Trail. It took a team of dogs about three weeks to travel across Alaska. They stopped at ‘roadhouses’ where the drivers could get a warm bed for the night and something to eat for both themselves and their dogs. The trail was used every winter until the 1920s when aeroplanes replaced steam boats and dog teams as the main form of transport.\n\nHowever, the dogs had one last taste of fame in 1925, when a disease called diphtheria hit the city of Nome. The disease could be cured, but, unfortunately, the closest medicine that could be found was in Anchorage, right on the other side of Alaska. Aeroplanes were still quite new, so no-one knew if they could fly in such cold weather. Also, Carl Eielson, the only pilot considered skilled enough to manage the flight, was away on a trip at the time. It was therefore agreed that the medicine would be transported using teams of dogs instead.\n\nThe trip covered over 1,000 kilometres, most of it along the Iditarod Trail. It took twenty teams just six days to complete the journey. Leonhard Seppala, a Norwegian who had come to Alaska looking for gold, travelled the first 400 of those kilometres. He had to trust his dog Togo's ability to find his way in the blinding snow and Togo turned out to be a reliable guide.\n\nThe last part of the journey was done by Gunnar Kaasen who had driven dog teams in Alaska for 21 years. His lead dog was Balto. At one point Balto refused to go any further, and saved the team from falling into icy water. The diphtheria was stopped and Balto became a hero.\n\nNowadays a dog sled race is held every year from Anchorage to Nome, following the route of that famous journey in 1925. It is called the Iditarod and has become known as ‘The Last Great Race on Earth’.","items":[{"n":11,"q":"The population of Alaska remained the same throughout the 1880s.","answer":"B"},{"n":12,"q":"For a short time, more people lived in Iditarod than in any other city in Alaska.","answer":"A"},{"n":13,"q":"After 1910, it became possible to deliver letters in winter as well as summer.","answer":"A"},{"n":14,"q":"When travelling on the Iditarod Trail, drivers had to take food for the dogs with them.","answer":"B"},{"n":15,"q":"In the 1920s, aeroplanes were used more often than boats and dogs.","answer":"A"},{"n":16,"q":"Doctors in Nome had a good supply of medicine to cure diphtheria.","answer":"B"},{"n":17,"q":"The pilot Carl Eielson refused to fly his plane because of the cold weather.","answer":"B"},{"n":18,"q":"Leonhard Seppala’s dog was able to lead him safely to his destination.","answer":"A"},{"n":19,"q":"Balto fell into some icy water but managed to save himself.","answer":"B"},{"n":20,"q":"The Iditarod race takes a different route every year.","answer":"B"}]},{"part":4,"title":"","text":"The painter Craigie Aitchison was born in Scotland. He came to London intending to study law, but went to art school instead. There he found the traditional drawing classes difficult, but still kept on painting.\n\nIn his late twenties he was given money by the Italian government to study art, and became interested in early Italian artists, which shows in some of his work. He loved the greens and browns of the Italian fields and the clear light there, and wanted to put this light into his paintings.\n\nThis led him to paint colours thinly one on top of another from light to dark, but he insists he’s never sure what the results will be. He says, ‘It’s a secret - because I don’t know myself. I don’t start by painting yellow, knowing I’m going to put anything on top.’ Like most talented people, Aitchison makes it sound easy. ‘Anyone can do the colours — you can buy them. I simply notice what you put the colours next to.’ Unlike some artists, he never does drawings before he starts a painting, as he feels that if he did, he might get bored and not do the painting afterwards. Instead, Aitchison changes his paintings many times before they are finished. This explains why his favourite models are people who don’t ask to see their pictures while he’s painting them. ‘If I feel they’re worried and want to look at the painting, I can’t do it.’\n\nSince moving to London years ago, he has not felt part of the Scottish painting scene. He says he is not interested in following any tradition, but just paints the way he can. However, his work still influences young British painters.","items":[{"n":21,"q":"What is the writer trying to do in the text?","options":["describe particular works by Craigie Aitchison","teach readers how to paint like Craigie Aitchison","introduce readers to the artist Craigie Aitchison","explain how Craigie Aitchison has made money from painting"],"answer":"C"},{"n":22,"q":"What can the reader learn about Aitchison from the text?","options":["He works in a different way from other artists.","He often gets bored with his paintings.","He improved his drawing by going to art school.","He did some paintings for the Italian government."],"answer":"A"},{"n":23,"q":"What does Aitchison say about his use of colour?","options":["He likes starting with the darkest colours first.","He knows the colours he’s aiming for when he begins.","He prefers to paint with yellows, greens, and browns.","He understands how different colours work together."],"answer":"D"},{"n":24,"q":"Aitchison prefers models who don’t","options":["keep talking to him while he’s working.","ask him about his strange method of working.","worry about how long the work will take.","feel anxious to see the work as it’s developing."],"answer":"D"},{"n":25,"q":"What might a visitor at an exhibition say about Aitchison’s work?","options":["I love his recent paintings of Scotland, which are very similar to a number of other Scottish painters.","You can still see the influence of his trip to Italy in some of these pictures.","You can tell he spent a lot of time drawing the picture before he started painting.","I wonder if his law training helps him at all, especially in selling his work."],"answer":"B"}]},{"part":5,"title":"ZOOS","text":"People began to (0) ……… (keep) animals in zoos [26] 3,000 years ago, when the rulers of China opened an enormous zoo called the Gardens of Intelligence. In many of the early zoos, animals [27] taught to perform for the visitors. This no longer [28] and it is accepted that the purpose of zoos is for people to see animals behaving naturally. Today, most cities have a zoo or wildlife park. However, not [29] approves of zoos. People who think that zoos are a good idea say they [30] us with the opportunity to [31] about the natural world and be close to wild animals. Both of [32] would not be possible [33] zoos. On the other hand, some people disapprove of zoos because they [34] it is wrong to put animals in cages and argue that in zoos which are not [35] properly, animals live in dirty conditions and eat unsuitable food.","gaps":{"26":{"options":["above","over","more","beyond"],"answer":"B"},"27":{"options":["are","have","were","had"],"answer":"C"},"28":{"options":["appears","becomes","develops","happens"],"answer":"D"},"29":{"options":["somebody","everybody","nobody","anybody"],"answer":"B"},"30":{"options":["produce","bring","provide","make"],"answer":"C"},"31":{"options":["discover","learn","find","realise"],"answer":"B"},"32":{"options":["that","what","whose","these"],"answer":"D"},"33":{"options":["without","instead","except","unless"],"answer":"A"},"34":{"options":["hope","expect","imagine","believe"],"answer":"D"},"35":{"options":["ordered","managed","decided","aimed"],"answer":"B"}}}],"writing":[{"part":1,"title":"Here are some sentences about a swimming pool.","items":[{"n":1,"first":"It’s six months since I last went swimming.","before":"I","after":"been swimming for six months.","answers":["have not","I have not"]},{"n":2,"first":"The new pool is near to my home.","before":"It’s not","after":"the new pool to my home.","answers":["far from","far away from","very far from","very far away from","too far from","too far away from","that far from","that far away from","so far from","so far away from"]},{"n":3,"first":"If you can’t swim, you’re not allowed in the deep end.","before":"You’re not allowed in the deep end","after":"you can swim.","answers":["unless","except if","until","till","before"]},{"n":4,"first":"My friend Sam wanted me to go swimming with him.","before":"‘Why","after":"come swimming with me?’ suggested Sam.","answers":["don't you","not"]},{"n":5,"first":"I didn’t go swimming with Sam because I was very busy.","before":"I was","after":"busy to go swimming with Sam.","answers":["too","really too","much too","far too","unfortunately too"]}]},{"part":2,"items":[{"n":6,"kind":"message","task":"A TV company came to your school yesterday to make a film.\n\nWrite an email to your English friend Alice. In your email, you should\n• explain why the TV company chose your school\n• tell her who or what they filmed\n• say when the programme will be shown on television.\n\nWrite 35–45 words on your answer sheet.","points":["dire perché è stata scelta la sua scuola","dire chi o che cosa è stato filmato","dire quando si potrà vedere il programma in TV"]}]},{"part":3,"items":[{"n":7,"kind":"letter","task":"This is part of a letter you receive from an English friend.\n\nMy parents want me to go on holiday with them this summer but I’d prefer to go somewhere with my friends. I have to choose. What do you think I should do?\n\nNow write a letter, giving your friend some advice.\nWrite your letter on your answer sheet."},{"n":8,"kind":"story","task":"Your English teacher has asked you to write a story for homework.\nYour story must begin with this sentence:\n\nAs soon as I saw the handwriting on the envelope I smiled.\n\nWrite your story on your answer sheet."}]}],"listening":[{"part":1,"audio":"audio/pet6-t2-l1.mp3","items":[{"n":1,"q":"Where are the dictionaries?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":2,"q":"Which evening dress does the woman decide to wear?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"A"},{"n":3,"q":"What is the man’s job now?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":4,"q":"Which calendar will the boy buy?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"C"},{"n":5,"q":"What time will the writer arrive at the bookshop?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":6,"q":"What did the woman leave in the restaurant?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":7,"q":"Where is the bicycle?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"A"}],"example":"img/pet6-t2-l1-ex.jpg"},{"part":2,"intro":"You will hear a radio interview with Jack Williams, who is talking about a town called Swanton.","audio":"audio/pet6-t2-l2.mp3","items":[{"n":8,"q":"Where is the town of Swanton located?","options":["near the sea","by a lake","on a hill"],"answer":"A"},{"n":9,"q":"What does Jack like most about living in Swanton?","options":["There are opportunities for climbing nearby.","There is plenty of activity in the town.","There is interesting wildlife near the town."],"answer":"A"},{"n":10,"q":"What does Jack say about entertainment in Swanton?","options":["A music festival takes place in the town.","Its football club has done well this year.","An arts centre has recently opened."],"answer":"C"},{"n":11,"q":"Jack is worried about the environment of Swanton because","options":["the water in the river is dirty.","there are few wild birds around today.","pollution has destroyed the plants in one area."],"answer":"B"},{"n":12,"q":"What does Jack say about the way Swanton has changed?","options":["He preferred the town when it was smaller.","He thinks it is a more interesting place.","He is sorry that there are fewer jobs available."],"answer":"B"},{"n":13,"q":"Jack is positive about the future of Swanton because","options":["there is a successful new shopping centre.","there will soon be a new airport.","a new university is opening."],"answer":"A"}]},{"part":3,"intro":"You will hear a woman talking on the radio about a singing course she attended.","audio":"audio/pet6-t2-l3.mp3","notes":"Singing for Beginners\n\nPlace: Brownstoke College\n\nCourse details:\n• Lena Phipps, a very good former [14] singer is the tutor\n• the maximum number of students per course is [15]\n• all classes start with exercises that help students to [16]\n• students learn to sing [17], modern and pop songs\n• accommodation is in single or twin rooms\n• cooked breakfast, lunch and dinner are included\n• there’s a very good lunch, especially [18]\n\nDate the next course starts: [19] 24th September","answers":{"14":["opera","operas"],"15":["ten","10","ten students","10 students"],"16":["relax","to relax"],"17":["classic","classical","classics","classic songs","classical songs"],"18":["salad","salads","the salad","salad bar","the salad bar","salads bar"],"19":["Tuesday","Tues"]}},{"part":4,"intro":"You will hear a conversation between a man, Marco, and his wife, Sarah, about a film they have just seen at the cinema.","audio":"audio/pet6-t2-l4.mp3","items":[{"n":20,"q":"Sarah was expecting to enjoy the film.","answer":"B"},{"n":21,"q":"Marco and Sarah agree that the city in the film was London.","answer":"A"},{"n":22,"q":"Marco feels that the length of the film made it rather boring.","answer":"B"},{"n":23,"q":"Sarah was upset about how some of the audience behaved during the film.","answer":"A"},{"n":24,"q":"Sarah was disappointed with the way the main actor performed.","answer":"B"},{"n":25,"q":"Marco thinks this film is the best the director has made.","answer":"B"}]}]};
